/-!
# The small window `[6, 469 615]` of `eq:fraiture-small`, by one kernel certificate

`Eq_FraitureSmall` (EP1054 lines 728–755) asks that every `N ∈ [6, 10^7]` be represented. Its
upper part `[469 616, 10^7]` lies inside the first prime window `Eq_FraitureFirstWindow`
(`[469 616, 273 803 744 799 154]`), so only `[6, 469 615]` needs the paper's small sieve. On that
range this file checks the sieve in the kernel (`smallChk_cert`, one `decide +kernel`) and so
proves check 1 of `verify.rs` restricted to it (`smallSieveMarked_le`), with no input.

## The certificate

* **`j = 1`.** For the 93 values of `smallBs` (`1 ≤ B ≤ 1000`, chosen greedily offline) the
  integers `σ(B) + q`, `q > B` prime, are marked in bulk against the prime bitset
  `primeBits 469 615`. They cover every integer of `[6, 469 615]` except `7` and 621 even numbers
  (the full range `1 ≤ B ≤ 1000` covers exactly the same set: the other 907 values add nothing).
* **`j ≥ 2`.** Each of those 621 numbers has a witness `(B, d, q)` in `smallWs`:
  `N = σ(B) + q · Fdiv B d`, `d ∣ B`, `d ≥ 2` (68 distinct pairs `(B, d)`, all `B ≤ 221`).
* **`7`** is the one unmarked integer (EP1054 line 754; `7 = 1 + 2 + 4` from `m = 4`).

The lists were produced offline (numpy sieve); the proof depends on none of that search, because
every fact it uses is re-checked by the kernel: primality through `testBit_primeBits`, `σ(B)` and
the prefix sums through `dsum_eq_Fdiv`.

**Measured (v4.31.0, 2026-09-26):** `smallChk_cert` adds ~8 s CPU and ~0.85 GB peak working set
over the file's import baseline (23 s, 1.6 GB); the whole file checks in ~34 s at 2.45 GB. Its
footprint is `[propext]`.
-/

